ce:section.ce:para:
As shown in Fig. 2
                           , the trends of the total concentration of VFAs and ethanol during the entire DF stage were clearly divided into two parts: an upward trend from the start-up to day 3 or day 4, and followed by a downward trend untill the end of DF process. This phenomenon corresponds with the results reported by Shi et al. [23] and Tian et al. [31]. The total VFA and ethanol concentrations recorded in RTCK were in the range of 5892.9–9481.4 mg·L−1. The highest total concentrations of VFA and ethanol obtained in RT50, RT80, RT110, and RT140 were 10633.7, 10771.4, 11925.1, and 12517.2 mg·L−1, respectively, resulting in the improvement of 12.2–32.0% when compared to RTCK. The results indicated that the HTP temperature had a positive impact on acidification performance, which could be explained by the improved VS removal rate (Table 2
                           ). Notably, hydrogen production almost ceased from the 2nd day in the DF stage, however, the total concentration of VFAs and ethanol increased before it decreased, warranting further study from the perspective of microorganisms. Furthermore, in comparison with that of RTCK (7214.7 mg·L−1), the total concentration of VFAs and ethanol in reactors with HTP substrates at day 7 was reduced by 5.4–25.9%. A higher total concentration of VFAs and ethanol generally provides more intermediates for biomethane generation, ultimately resulting in higher biomethane production [12].
